title: Example - Markdown image - body override
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Markdown

The [IMAGE marker](#vocab/markdown/IMAGE) follows CommonMark mapping: `![alt](src "tooltip")` — body becomes `alt`, the trailing `"..."` parameter becomes `tooltip`. When either is absent, the source tiddler's `alt-text` field fills in. Plus a smart accessibility rule: short user-alt (1–2 words) yields to a 3+ word source `alt-text`.

## Priority resolution

For each of `alt` and `tooltip`, the engine walks this decision:

1. Did the user supply a value at the callsite? (body for `alt`, `"..."` parameter for `tooltip`)
2. If yes → use the user's value (default).
3. If no → does the source tiddler exist AND have the `alt-text` field set? Use that.
4. ''Special, alt only:'' if the user supplied an alt of 1–2 words AND the source `alt-text` is 3+ words, the source wins regardless of step 2. Descriptive accessibility text beats a brief callsite label.
5. Nothing on either side → attribute not emitted.

## Pattern 1 — user body becomes alt

![Motovun Jack on a stone wall](https://tiddlywiki.com/favicon.ico)

```
![Motovun Jack on a stone wall](https://tiddlywiki.com/favicon.ico)
```

URL source, no source-tiddler lookup. User's body (6 words) becomes the rendered `alt`. No tooltip supplied → tooltip empty.

## Pattern 2 — explicit tooltip parameter

![Favicon](https://tiddlywiki.com/favicon.ico "Hover text from the user")

```
![Favicon](https://tiddlywiki.com/favicon.ico "Hover text from the user")
```

Body → `alt`, `"..."` → `tooltip`. Hover the image; the tooltip shows.

## Pattern 3 — empty body, source field fills both alt and tooltip

![](#Motovun Jack.jpg)

```
![](#Motovun Jack.jpg)
```

Empty body, no `"..."`. Source tiddler [Motovun Jack.jpg](#Motovun Jack.jpg) has an `alt-text` field → both `alt` and `tooltip` come from that field via the fallback rule.

## Pattern 4 — short user-alt loses to richer source `alt-text`

![Pic](#Motovun Jack.jpg)

```
![Pic](#Motovun Jack.jpg)
```

User wrote `Pic` (1 word) as body. Source tiddler's `alt-text` is several words long. The smart-alt rule kicks in: the rendered `alt` becomes the source's `alt-text`, not `Pic`. Tooltip also comes from the source field (no `"..."` supplied). Hover to see.

## Pattern 5 — descriptive user-alt wins over source

![A photograph of Motovun Jack sleeping on a stone wall in Croatia](#Motovun Jack.jpg)

```
![A photograph of Motovun Jack sleeping on a stone wall in Croatia](#Motovun Jack.jpg)
```

User's alt is 12 words — well above the 2-word threshold. Smart-alt rule doesn't fire. The rendered `alt` is the user's body. Tooltip falls back to source `alt-text` (no `"..."` parameter).

## Why this priority order

- Authors should be able to override at the callsite when the context demands a different label (Pattern 1, 2, 5).
- Source-tiddler fields are an authoritative default (Pattern 3) — write the description ONCE per image, reuse it at every callsite.
- The smart-alt rule (Pattern 4) catches the easy mistake: a one-word "Pic" or "Image" label that's worthless for screen readers gets replaced by the proper description from the image's own metadata.

